# 6. Early Education: Powerful in the Right Form

Early education creates perhaps the most difficult intervention puzzle of all.

Two landmark programs—Perry Preschool and the Abecedarian Project—demonstrated that intensive early educational environments can influence trajectories far beyond the end of the intervention.

Perry produced relatively modest or fading effects on measured IQ but enduring differences in outcomes such as education, employment, crime, health, and income. Abecedarian produced durable cognitive and educational advantages alongside later health effects. :contentReference[oaicite:16]{index=16}

These studies changed the meaning of “success.”

A developmental intervention does not need to preserve an advantage on every cognitive test for decades to matter.

A short-lived cognitive difference can coexist with a long-lived change in life trajectory.

But this does not mean that every preschool program reproduces the results of these landmark projects.

### Efficacy is not effectiveness

Modern scaled preschool programs reveal the difficulty.

The Tennessee Voluntary Pre-K evaluation produced the expected short-term gains in early academic readiness. Yet later follow-up showed convergence and, in some outcomes, poorer academic and disciplinary results among children assigned to the program. The Head Start Impact Study likewise found that early cognitive and language gains largely converged with control-group outcomes over subsequent schooling. :contentReference[oaicite:17]{index=17}

These results should not be reduced to the claim that “preschool is harmful.”

The comparison instead raises a more fundamental question:

> **What did the successful programs actually provide, and was that developmental environment reproduced at scale?**

The research review notes differences in instructional style and classroom process between intensive model programs and scaled systems. It also distinguishes structural quality—class size, ratios, credentials—from **process quality**, meaning what actually happens during interactions between adults and children. :contentReference[oaicite:18]{index=18}

This distinction closely echoes earlier chapters.

A developmental environment is not defined only by its architecture.

It is defined by what the child experiences inside it.

